      "tool_uses": [],
      "assistant_text": "Perfect. Now let me answer your question:\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are two applications of the same architectural pattern: **centralizing critical startup decisions at a single composition point**.\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** ensures the Anthropic API key is injected into the child process environment at spawn time \u2014 in exactly one place \u2014 rather than allowing multiple parts of the codebase to read or set credentials. A compromised Hub process cannot leak the key because it never holds it.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** applies the same principle to file-system locations: every runtime path (wiki root, database, secrets file, agent directories) is resolved in a single path resolver at startup, consuming unified configuration. No other part of the codebase discovers locations on its own.\n\n**The connection**: In both cases, you gain **correctness and auditability by knowing there is exactly one place where the decision is made**. You cannot accidentally leak a credential from code that never reads it; you cannot accidentally use the wrong path when all paths flow through a single validator. Adding a new credential or location means extending one composition point, not searching through dozens of places.\n\nThis pattern is particularly powerful because both credentials and paths are decisions that should remain locked in once the process starts \u2014 they are deterministically derived at startup and then stable throughout the process\u0027s lifetime.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], so the insight is preserved for future reference.",
